<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Glass material colours that are not part of the HarmonyOS palette itself.

  The ArkUI design is a real `backgroundEffect` blur, so it never needs to pick a highlight colour —
  the light simply comes from whatever is behind the surface. On Android a plain View cannot sample
  what is behind it, so the light has to be painted, and the paint has to be a colour the design
  system can adjust per theme:
    - `glass_sheen_top`   the specular highlight along the top edge of a surface;
    - `glass_sheen_mid`   the centre of the falloff;
    - `glass_sheen_rim`   the bevelled rim, brighter than the flat border it replaces.
  White at a low alpha reads as "light reflecting off glass" in light mode; in dark mode the same
  white would wash the palette out, so it is tinted toward the accent instead (see values-night).
-->
<resources>
    <color name="glass_sheen_top">#38FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="glass_sheen_mid">#0DFFFFFF</color>
    <color name="glass_sheen_rim">#FFEFF9F5</color>

    <!--
      Dock tab states, light theme.

      The selected wash is the accent at low alpha so the active tab reads as a lens over the glass
      rather than a solid block; the ripple is the same accent, stronger. The alphas were chosen by
      compositing rather than by eye: at 14% the wash lands on #DAE5E2 against the glass's #FAFBFB
      and is effectively invisible, at 30% it gives #B5CCC5 (a tinted lens, still not a solid block),
      and 40% gives #A9C6BE — clearly selected without turning the pill into a filled button.

      These must be theme-aware: a single hard-coded light-theme value composites to a dull
      grey-purple over the dark glass, which is what made the selected tab look dirty in dark mode.
    -->
    <color name="dock_tab_selected">#66176149</color>
    <color name="dock_tab_ripple">#80176149</color>
</resources>
